    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The introduction of ionizable lipids with tuned pKa values (typically 6.5–7.0) facilitates critical protonation dynamics within the acidic endosomal pH microenvironment, inducing a transmembrane potential gradient that drives endocytic vesicle rupture and cytosolic release of mRNA. Simultaneously, manipulating the nitrogen-to-phosphate (N/P) ratio governs the supramolecular assembly of the RNA:lipid complex, optimizing the charge neutralization required to stabilize the polyanionic nucleic acid and maximize transfection complex internalization into hepatocytes without premature release in the bloodstream. To further refine therapeutic efficacy, the substitution of uridine (U) with pseudouridine (Ψ) nucleosides attenuates recognition by TLR7 and TLR8 receptors, thereby disrupting the TGF-β and NF-κB signaling cascades that typically initiate potent Type I interferon production. This molecular suppression prevents TRAM-mediated antiviral pathway activation, ensuring that mRNA translation proceeds with minimal innate immune surveillance, which is essential for generating sustained, high-viability antigenic expression profiles in vaccine platforms without triggering debilitating systemic inflammation.",
      "source": "target",
